<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Massachusetts Institute of Technology, Cambridge, MA
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Kayhan Behdin
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>LinkedIn, Sunnyvale, CA
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Zhipeng Wang
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>LinkedIn, Sunnyvale, CA
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Shao Tang
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>LinkedIn, Sunnyvale, CA
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Qingquan Song
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>LinkedIn, Sunnyvale, CA
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Rahul Mazumder
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>LinkedIn, Sunnyvale, CA
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Massachusetts Institute of Technology, Cambridge, MA
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Reasoning language models such as DeepSeek-R1 produce long chain-of-thought traces during inference time which make them costly to deploy at scale. We show that using compression techniques such as neural network pruning produces greater performance loss than in typical language modeling tasks, and in some cases can make the model <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">slower</span> since they cause the model to produce more thinking tokens but with worse performance. We show that this is partly due to the fact that standard LLM pruning methods often focus on input reconstruction, whereas reasoning is a decode-dominated task. We introduce a simple, drop-in fix: during pruning we jointly reconstruct activations from the input and the model’s on-policy chain-of-thought traces. This “Reasoning-Aware Compression” (RAC) integrates seamlessly into existing pruning workflows such as SparseGPT, and boosts their performance significantly. Code reproducing the results in the paper can be found at: <a href="https://github.com/RyanLucas3/RAC" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/RyanLucas3/RAC</a></p>
  
</div>
<div id="id2" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_align_bottom">
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:2.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:2.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">A Preprint
<br class="ltx_break"></span></p>
<table id="p1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models with step-by-step reasoning abilities have become essential for solving complex, multi-step tasks in domains such as mathematics, coding, and logical reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Reasoning models generate explicit chains-of-thought (intermediate reasoning steps) that significantly improve accuracy on challenging benchmarks, but at the cost of producing very long outputs for each query. For example, the open-source DeepSeek-R1 model (671B parameters) achieves strong reasoning performance but must output lengthy explanation traces, making it extremely resource-intensive to deploy at scale<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>. This has motivated extensive research into model compression techniques, especially pruning and quantization, to reduce the memory footprint and inference latency of large reasoning LLMs<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Pruning removes redundant weights or neurons from the network <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Frantar &amp; Alistarh, 2023a</a>)</cite>, while quantization reduces the precision of model weights (e.g. using 8-bit or 4-bit instead of 16-bit)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Frantar et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, and both approaches can drastically shrink model size. Effective compression is critical for integrating reasoning-enabled models into real-world workflows (for instance, deploying them on commodity hardware or within high-throughput agent pipelines) without prohibitive cost. However, applying standard compression pipelines to reasoning LLMs is challenging: popular pruning and quantization methods are typically tuned to preserve general language modeling performance, and they can lead to disproportionately large accuracy drops on complex reasoning tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As an example, we prune the DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI et al. (2025)</a></cite> checkpoint with SparseGPT using a single-pass C4 calibration set of 1M tokens.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:39%;"><img src="2509.12464v1/Acc_vs_runtime.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:191/127;" width="191" height="127" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Pruning hurts both accuracy <em id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">and</em> runtime on MATH-500.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">At sparsity levels of 30%, 50%, and 70%, we evaluate each pruned model on the MATH-500 benchmark (zero-shot, 32k max tokens). As sparsity increases, MATH-500 accuracy falls while total evaluation time grows sharply. Counter-intuitively, heavy pruning also <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">slows down</span> inference because the model produces much longer chains of thought, rambling more yet answering less accurately. Since the goal of compression is to maintain accuracy while reducing inference time, this is obviously not desirable.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Background</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reasoning models.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Conventional LLMs are trained to maximise the conditional likelihood
<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\theta}(y_{0:L-1}\mid x)=\prod_{t=0}^{L-1}p_{\theta}\!\bigl(y_{t}\mid x,y_{&lt;t}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(y_{0:L-1}\mid x)=\prod_{t=0}^{L-1}p_{\theta}\!\bigl(y_{t}\mid x,y_{&lt;t}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>
of an output sequence <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathcal{V}^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathcal{V}^{L}</annotation></semantics></math> of length <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> from the vocabulary <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> given a prompt <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. A <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">reasoning model</em> instead produces:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(c_{0:T-1},\,y_{0:L-1}),\qquad\text{with}\;c_{0:T-1}\in\mathcal{V}^{T},\;y_{0:L-1}\in\mathcal{V}^{L}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mtext>with</mtext><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c_{0:T-1},\,y_{0:L-1}),\qquad\text{with}\;c_{0:T-1}\in\mathcal{V}^{T},\;y_{0:L-1}\in\mathcal{V}^{L},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> is a chain-of-thought (CoT) of length <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> is the final
answer e.g. a single numeric value, a complete proof, or a code block.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al. (2022)</a></cite> for
evidence that even noisy CoT traces boost reasoning accuracy.</span></span></span> While a conventional LLM can be prompted to emit a CoT <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> before producing its answer <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, reasoning models are explicitly trained so that the generated chain and answer yield a verifiable task reward <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\bigl(x,c,y\bigr)\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\bigl(x,c,y\bigr)\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, for example, exact-match grading on math, unit-test passes for code, or logical consistency checks for formal proofs.
This has recently become popularized by the <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeepSeek-R1</span> reasoning model, which is optimized via Group-Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI et al. (2025)</a></cite>. The combined CoT and answer distribution is modeled as:
<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(c,y\mid x)=\prod_{t=0}^{T-1}\pi_{\theta}(c_{t}\mid x,c_{&lt;t})\;\pi_{\theta}(y\mid x,c_{0:T-1})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(c,y\mid x)=\prod_{t=0}^{T-1}\pi_{\theta}(c_{t}\mid x,c_{&lt;t})\;\pi_{\theta}(y\mid x,c_{0:T-1}).</annotation></semantics></math>
Given a pre-trained LLM with parameters <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, GRPO fine-tunes <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> by drawing <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> full trajectories
<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(c^{(k)},y^{(k)})\}_{k=1}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(c^{(k)},y^{(k)})\}_{k=1}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math> for the same prompt <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>,
evaluating a scalar task reward
<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{k}=R\bigl(x,c^{(k)},y^{(k)}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{k}=R\bigl(x,c^{(k)},y^{(k)}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>,
and computing the clipped policy-gradient loss:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{GRPO}}(\theta)=-\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}\operatorname{clip}\!\bigl(\rho_{k}(\theta),1-\varepsilon,1+\varepsilon\bigr)\,\bigl(r_{k}-\bar{r}\bigr),\qquad\bar{r}=\tfrac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}r_{k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>GRPO</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><mpadded style="width:1.428em;" width="1.428em"><mi>clip</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.170em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac></mstyle><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{GRPO}}(\theta)=-\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}\operatorname{clip}\!\bigl(\rho_{k}(\theta),1-\varepsilon,1+\varepsilon\bigr)\,\bigl(r_{k}-\bar{r}\bigr),\qquad\bar{r}=\tfrac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}r_{k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{k}(\theta)\;=\;\frac{\pi_{\theta}\!\bigl(c^{(k)},y^{(k)}\mid x\bigr)}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}\!\bigl(c^{(k)},y^{(k)}\mid x\bigr)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.710em" minsize="1.710em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo maxsize="1.710em" minsize="1.710em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>old</mtext></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.710em" minsize="1.710em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo maxsize="1.710em" minsize="1.710em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{k}(\theta)\;=\;\frac{\pi_{\theta}\!\bigl(c^{(k)},y^{(k)}\mid x\bigr)}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}\!\bigl(c^{(k)},y^{(k)}\mid x\bigr)}</annotation></semantics></math> is ratio describing the probability of response <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> in the new policy <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> (being optimized) relative to the current policy <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\text{old}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>old</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\text{old}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, when <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{k}&gt;\bar{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{k}&gt;\bar{r}</annotation></semantics></math> i.e. the <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th response generated higher reward than its group mean, the policy will be updated in favor of this response, and vice versa. This process of generating long thought chains before outputting an answer can be viewed as training the model to perform search i.e. to try out multiple paths towards a solution before deciding on an answer. This is made explicit by generalizations of CoT such as tree-of-thoughts, which explicitly enforce a search-like procedure <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al. (2023)</a></cite>. Consequently, a longer search process (or higher number of inference tokens), has been associated with more accurate answers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI (2024)</a></cite>. While this makes these models more effective, it also means they incur substantial inference latency  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">LLM compression methods.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Modern language models contain billions of parameters, so <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">compression</em>
is widely used for reducing GPU memory footprint, inference latency, and
energy cost while preserving most of the model’s accuracy. Given a frozen dense model with layer weights <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{1},\dots,\mathbf{W}_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{1},\dots,\mathbf{W}_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> and activations <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{\ell}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\ell}\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{\ell}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\ell}\times N}</annotation></semantics></math> for layer <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, several LLM compression methods (e.g. SparseGPT for pruning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Frantar &amp; Alistarh (2023b)</a></cite>, GPTQ for quantization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Frantar et al. (2023)</a></cite>) solve a problem of the form: find compressed weights <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathbf{W}}_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathbf{W}}_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> by solving, independently for each layer,</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{\;\widehat{\mathbf{W}}_{\ell}\in\mathcal{W}}\;\bigl\|\mathbf{W}_{\ell}\mathbf{X}_{\ell}-\widehat{\mathbf{W}}_{\ell}\,\mathbf{X}_{\ell}\bigr\|_{2}^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.280em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\;\widehat{\mathbf{W}}_{\ell}\in\mathcal{W}}\;\bigl\|\mathbf{W}_{\ell}\mathbf{X}_{\ell}-\widehat{\mathbf{W}}_{\ell}\,\mathbf{X}_{\ell}\bigr\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of compressed weights, for example in unstructured pruning <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}=\{\widehat{\mathbf{W}}\mid\|\widehat{\mathbf{W}}\|_{0}\leq S\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}=\{\widehat{\mathbf{W}}\mid\|\widehat{\mathbf{W}}\|_{0}\leq S\}</annotation></semantics></math>. In quantization, <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}</annotation></semantics></math> could be a set of 4 or 8-bit matrices.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The calibration data.</span>
In <a href="#S2.E1" title="1 ‣ LLM compression methods. ‣ 2 Background ‣ Reasoning Models Can Be Accurately Pruned via Chain-of-Thought Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation 1</span></a><math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext=",\mathbf{X}_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">,\mathbf{X}_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> is the so-called calibration data, and for LLMs a text corpus of size <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> tokens comprises the column dimension of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>. The calibration data is typically chosen to mimic the general distribution of natural language, e.g. the C4 dataset is a common choice. For standard LLMs, the calibration data is typically derived from a set
of inputs (or prompts) <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. Concretely, let <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{0:N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0:N-1}</annotation></semantics></math> be a batch of
<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> prompt tokens from the calibration corpus and let
<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times|\mathcal{V}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times|\mathcal{V}|}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the embedding matrix.
Define the layer-wise hidden states for each token by:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(0)}\;=\;E\,\mathbf{e}_{x_{t}},\qquad\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)}\;=\;f_{j}\bigl(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j-1)}\bigr),\;j=1,\dots,\ell-1," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mi>E</mi><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(0)}\;=\;E\,\mathbf{e}_{x_{t}},\qquad\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j)}\;=\;f_{j}\bigl(\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(j-1)}\bigr),\;j=1,\dots,\ell-1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{\text{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>j</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{\text{th}}</annotation></semantics></math> transformer layer (including
attention, MLP, residual connections, etc.), and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}_{x_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}_{x_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the embedding for token <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. The states are stacked
column-wise to obtain the calibration activation matrix:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{\ell}\;=\;\bigl[\,\mathbf{x}_{0}^{(\ell-1)},\;\mathbf{x}_{1}^{(\ell-1)},\;\dots,\;\mathbf{x}_{N-1}^{(\ell-1)}\,\bigr]\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\ell}\times N}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.170em">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{\ell}\;=\;\bigl[\,\mathbf{x}_{0}^{(\ell-1)},\;\mathbf{x}_{1}^{(\ell-1)},\;\dots,\;\mathbf{x}_{N-1}^{(\ell-1)}\,\bigr]\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\ell}\times N},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">so the <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-th token embedding (processed after <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell-1</annotation></semantics></math> transformer
layers) is exactly the vector that will enter layer <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> when the
dense model processes token <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. This
<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> is what standard pruning/quantisation algorithms use
to measure the reconstruction error of the compressed weight matrix
<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathbf{W}}_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathbf{W}}_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This setup aligns with typical LLM workloads where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|x|\!\gg\!|y|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">≫</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|x|\!\gg\!|y|</annotation></semantics></math> (long context, short reply).
By contrast, in reasoning LMs we often observe <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|c|+|y|\!\gg\!|x|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">≫</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|c|+|y|\!\gg\!|x|</annotation></semantics></math> as the CoT dominates the token budget; consequently, calibrating solely on prompts risks a deployment-time distribution shift, where activations are driven primarily by CoT tokens rather than inputs.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Benchmarking compressed reasoning LLMs.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Concurrently with our work, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2025b)</a></cite> run an extensive
evaluation of compressed DeepSeek-R1 variants.
They apply SparseGPT to student models distilled from Qwen and LLaMA,
but follow the default C4-style calibration pipeline (details of the
calibration set are not reported). Their results are similar to what we
observe: when using a generic dataset, accuracy on complex reasoning tasks
drops sharply with sparsity, chains of thought become repetitive or degenerate, and longer post-compression outputs correlate with lower task
accuracy.
Unlike their empirical study, our RAC method modifies the
calibration distribution itself by injecting on-policy CoT activations, and thereby mitigates the same performance loss during pruning. Recent work by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2025)</a></cite> shows that weight-only
W8A8 or mixed-precision W4A16 quantisation incurs no measurable
accuracy loss on several reasoning benchmarks, but they leave the
interaction with pruning largely unexplored.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Calibration data for post-training pruning.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Most single-pass pruning algorithms (e.g. SparseGPT, WANDA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al. (2024)</a></cite>, ALPS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meng et al. (2024)</a></cite>) rely on a small calibration set such as C4 to minimise layer-wise reconstruction loss. A recent paper reports that this choice is far from optimal. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bandari et al. (2024)</a></cite> compare seven pre-training and downstream datasets and finding large accuracy differences after pruning, and that across tasks C4 is rarely the optimal choice of calibration dataset. PPC-GPT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fan et al., 2025</a>)</cite> distils pruned student models with synthetic CoT traces, but still computes pruning scores on standard C4 activations. In contrast, we inject CoT activations directly into the SparseGPT objective, eliminating the separate distillation stage.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Reasoning-Aware Compression</h2>

<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Inference in an autoregressive reasoning model.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=(x_{0},\dots,x_{T_{\mathrm{in}}-1})\in\mathcal{V}^{T_{\mathrm{in}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>in</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>in</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=(x_{0},\dots,x_{T_{\mathrm{in}}-1})\in\mathcal{V}^{T_{\mathrm{in}}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a prompt to an autoregressive reasoning model, which could be a problem of math, coding, etc., and let <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the dense model’s conditional distribution over the vocabulary <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>. At inference, a reasoning model processes and generates a completed sequence:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="z_{0:T+L}=\big(x_{0},\dots,x_{T_{\mathrm{in}}-1},\,c_{T_{\mathrm{in}}},\dots,c_{T},\,y_{T+1},\dots,y_{T+L}\big)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>in</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>in</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{0:T+L}=\big(x_{0},\dots,x_{T_{\mathrm{in}}-1},\,c_{T_{\mathrm{in}}},\dots,c_{T},\,y_{T+1},\dots,y_{T+L}\big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which includes the prompt, the chain-of-thought (CoT) tokens <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, and the final answer tokens <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>. We partition the indices into
<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}=\{0,\dots,T_{\mathrm{in}}-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>in</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}=\{0,\dots,T_{\mathrm{in}}-1\}</annotation></semantics></math> being the prompt indices and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{T_{\mathrm{in}},\dots,T+L\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>in</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{T_{\mathrm{in}},\dots,T+L\}</annotation></semantics></math> being the decode indices. Each generated token <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (either <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>) is drawn autoregressively,</p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="z_{t}\sim\pi_{\theta}(\,\cdot\mid z_{0:t-1}\,),\qquad t\in\mathcal{D}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}\sim\pi_{\theta}(\,\cdot\mid z_{0:t-1}\,),\qquad t\in\mathcal{D}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">To compute the generated token, for each step <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the model first produces an embedding for the current token as:</p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(0)}=E\,e_{z_{t}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{1}},\quad t\in\mathcal{P}\cup\mathcal{D}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(0)}=E\,e_{z_{t}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{1}},\quad t\in\mathcal{P}\cup\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">and processes it through <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> transformer blocks computing:</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(\ell)}=f_{\ell}\!\left(\{\mathbf{x}_{\tau}^{(\ell-1)}\}_{\tau\leq t}\right),\qquad\ell=1,\dots,L,\quad t\in\mathcal{P}\cup\mathcal{D}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(\ell)}=f_{\ell}\!\left(\{\mathbf{x}_{\tau}^{(\ell-1)}\}_{\tau\leq t}\right),\qquad\ell=1,\dots,L,\quad t\in\mathcal{P}\cup\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-th transformer block. The hidden states that are required for decoding are thus:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\big\{\,\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(\ell)}\;:\;\ell\in\{1,\dots,L\},\;t\in\mathcal{P}\cup\mathcal{D}\,\big\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.558em">:</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big\{\,\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(\ell)}\;:\;\ell\in\{1,\dots,L\},\;t\in\mathcal{P}\cup\mathcal{D}\,\big\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">That is, crucially, to generated the complete sequence, the model relies on the activations that are computed on the input, but also on activations that arise from its own self-generated tokens. Once the final hidden state <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(L)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(L)}</annotation></semantics></math> is computed, it is mapped to vocabulary logits via the output projection <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\mathrm{out}}\in\mathbb{R}^{|\mathcal{V}|\times d_{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>out</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\mathrm{out}}\in\mathbb{R}^{|\mathcal{V}|\times d_{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> given by <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}_{t}=W_{\mathrm{out}}\,\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(L)}\in\mathbb{R}^{|\mathcal{V}|}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>out</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}_{t}=W_{\mathrm{out}}\,\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(L)}\in\mathbb{R}^{|\mathcal{V}|}.</annotation></semantics></math> which gives the next token distribution <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid z_{0:t})=\mathrm{softmax}(\bm{y}_{t})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>softmax</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid z_{0:t})=\mathrm{softmax}(\bm{y}_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Aligning compression with decoding during offline calibration.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose we have <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> calibration prompts <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x^{(m)}\}_{m=1}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x^{(m)}\}_{m=1}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, with corresponding prompt index sets <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and decode index sets <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. Standard post-training compression collects activations only for <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in\mathcal{P}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in\mathcal{P}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. only from the fixed prompt tokens in each sequence. For typical LLM applications, this is natural since in most settings <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{P}_{m}|\gg|\mathcal{D}_{m}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≫</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{P}_{m}|\gg|\mathcal{D}_{m}|</annotation></semantics></math> (long context, short reply), so the majority of inference-time activations come from the prompt, and the few decode-time activations that exist are often just noisy continuations that act as proxies for the original prompt distribution, for example, in autocomplete tasks where the completion closely mirrors the previous context. In RAC, since in reasoning tasks typically <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{D}_{m}|\gg|\mathcal{P}_{m}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≫</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{D}_{m}|\gg|\mathcal{P}_{m}|</annotation></semantics></math>, we modify this procedure by self-generating tokens during calibration to simulate the activations observed at decoding time. At each decode step <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the model’s own prediction is immediately re-used as the next input:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="z_{t+1}^{(m)}\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid z_{0:t}^{(m)}),\qquad\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid z_{0:t}^{(m)})=\mathrm{softmax}\!\big(W_{\mathrm{out}}\,\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(L,m)}\big),\quad t\in\mathcal{D}_{m}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mpadded style="width:3.720em;" width="3.720em"><mi>softmax</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>out</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t+1}^{(m)}\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid z_{0:t}^{(m)}),\qquad\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid z_{0:t}^{(m)})=\mathrm{softmax}\!\big(W_{\mathrm{out}}\,\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(L,m)}\big),\quad t\in\mathcal{D}_{m},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S4.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t+1}^{(0,m)}=E\,e_{z_{t+1}^{(m)}},\qquad\mathbf{x}_{t+1}^{(\ell,m)}=f_{\ell}\!\left(\{\mathbf{x}_{\tau}^{(\ell-1,m)}\}_{\tau\leq t+1}\right),\quad\ell=1,\dots,L." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><msub><mi>e</mi><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></msub><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t+1}^{(0,m)}=E\,e_{z_{t+1}^{(m)}},\qquad\mathbf{x}_{t+1}^{(\ell,m)}=f_{\ell}\!\left(\{\mathbf{x}_{\tau}^{(\ell-1,m)}\}_{\tau\leq t+1}\right),\quad\ell=1,\dots,L.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which gives a new set of hidden states for reconstruction <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{x}_{t+1}^{(\ell)}\}_{\ell=1}^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{x}_{t+1}^{(\ell)}\}_{\ell=1}^{L}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The resulting layer-<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> input <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t+1}^{(\ell-1,m)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t+1}^{(\ell-1,m)}</annotation></semantics></math> is then appended as a new column to the decode-time activation matrix:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{\ell}^{\mathrm{D}}\;\leftarrow\;\big[\,\mathbf{X}_{\ell}^{\mathrm{D}}\;\;\mathbf{x}_{t+1}^{(\ell-1,m)}\,\big]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>D</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.170em">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>D</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{\ell}^{\mathrm{D}}\;\leftarrow\;\big[\,\mathbf{X}_{\ell}^{\mathrm{D}}\;\;\mathbf{x}_{t+1}^{(\ell-1,m)}\,\big],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">so that after all decode steps, the activation matrix:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{\ell}^{\mathrm{D}}=\big[\,\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(\ell-1,m)}\,\big]_{\begin{subarray}{c}t\in\mathcal{D}_{m}\\
m=1,\dots,M\end{subarray}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\ell}\times N_{\mathrm{D}}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>D</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.170em">[</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{\ell}^{\mathrm{D}}=\big[\,\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(\ell-1,m)}\,\big]_{\begin{subarray}{c}t\in\mathcal{D}_{m}\\
m=1,\dots,M\end{subarray}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\ell}\times N_{\mathrm{D}}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">contains the same sequence of activations that the model will encounter during generation, where <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathrm{D}}=\sum_{m=1}^{M}|\mathcal{D}_{m}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathrm{D}}=\sum_{m=1}^{M}|\mathcal{D}_{m}|</annotation></semantics></math>. The full calibration matrix concatenates both prompt and decode activations <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{\ell}^{\mathrm{RAC}}=\big[\,\mathbf{X}_{\ell}^{\mathrm{P}}\;\;\mathbf{X}_{\ell}^{\mathrm{D}}\,\big]\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\ell}\times(N_{\mathrm{P}}+N_{\mathrm{D}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>RAC</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.170em">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>P</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>D</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{\ell}^{\mathrm{RAC}}=\big[\,\mathbf{X}_{\ell}^{\mathrm{P}}\;\;\mathbf{X}_{\ell}^{\mathrm{D}}\,\big]\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\ell}\times(N_{\mathrm{P}}+N_{\mathrm{D}})}</annotation></semantics></math>. The RAC calibration loss for layer <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> is then:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|(\mathbf{W}_{\ell}-\widehat{\mathbf{W}}_{\ell})\,\mathbf{X}_{\ell}^{\mathrm{RAC}}\|_{F}^{2}=\sum_{m=1}^{M}\sum_{t\in\mathcal{P}_{m}\cup\mathcal{D}_{m}}\|(\mathbf{W}_{\ell}-\widehat{\mathbf{W}}_{\ell})\,\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(\ell-1,m)}\|_{2}^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>RAC</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></munder><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|(\mathbf{W}_{\ell}-\widehat{\mathbf{W}}_{\ell})\,\mathbf{X}_{\ell}^{\mathrm{RAC}}\|_{F}^{2}=\sum_{m=1}^{M}\sum_{t\in\mathcal{P}_{m}\cup\mathcal{D}_{m}}\|(\mathbf{W}_{\ell}-\widehat{\mathbf{W}}_{\ell})\,\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(\ell-1,m)}\|_{2}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.5" class="ltx_p">From a statistical viewpoint, the layerwise objective solved during RAC calibration is:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\min_{\widehat{\mathbf{W}}_{\ell}\in\mathcal{W}}\;\frac{1}{M}\sum_{m=1}^{M}\mathbb{E}_{\,z^{(m)}_{0:T_{m}}\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid x^{(m)})}\sum_{\ell=1}^{L}\|(\mathbf{W}_{\ell}-\widehat{\mathbf{W}}_{\ell})\,\mathbf{X}_{\ell}^{\mathrm{RAC}}\|_{F}^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow></munder><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>M</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>RAC</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\widehat{\mathbf{W}}_{\ell}\in\mathcal{W}}\;\frac{1}{M}\sum_{m=1}^{M}\mathbb{E}_{\,z^{(m)}_{0:T_{m}}\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid x^{(m)})}\sum_{\ell=1}^{L}\|(\mathbf{W}_{\ell}-\widehat{\mathbf{W}}_{\ell})\,\mathbf{X}_{\ell}^{\mathrm{RAC}}\|_{F}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.6" class="ltx_p">The key difference between standard prompt-only calibration and RAC is that RAC’s calibration set covers <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\big\{\,\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(\ell-1,m)}:\ell=1,\dots,L,\;t\in\mathcal{P}_{m}\cup\mathcal{D}_{m},\;m=1,\dots,M\,\big\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big\{\,\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(\ell-1,m)}:\ell=1,\dots,L,\;t\in\mathcal{P}_{m}\cup\mathcal{D}_{m},\;m=1,\dots,M\,\big\},</annotation></semantics></math> i.e. all activations used during the full on-policy rollout. This set is generated by the same autoregressive mechanism (<a href="#S4.E2" title="In Inference in an autoregressive reasoning model. ‣ 4 Reasoning-Aware Compression ‣ Reasoning Models Can Be Accurately Pruned via Chain-of-Thought Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>)–(<a href="#S4.E4" title="In Inference in an autoregressive reasoning model. ‣ 4 Reasoning-Aware Compression ‣ Reasoning Models Can Be Accurately Pruned via Chain-of-Thought Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) used at inference, so the empirical distribution over these activations during calibration matches the inference-time distribution. Because the activations are collected under the dense model’s own policy, the procedure is analogous to on-policy distillation in reinforcement learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agarwal et al. (2024)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="algorithm1" class="ltx_float ltx_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="algorithm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span>Reasoning-Aware Compression (RAC)</figcaption>
<div id="algorithm1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="algorithm1.4.1" class="ltx_listingline">
<span id="algorithm1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span id="algorithm1.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> </span><math id="algorithm1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x^{(m)}\}_{m=1}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x^{(m)}\}_{m=1}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math>: calibration prompts;
<math id="algorithm1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>: dense LM with <math id="algorithm1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> layers;
<math id="algorithm1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>: max decode length;
<math id="algorithm1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>: target sparsity
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.2" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.2.1" class="ltx_text"><span id="algorithm1.4.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output:</span> </span><math id="algorithm1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{f}</annotation></semantics></math>: compressed model
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.4.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Phase I: Activation collection</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.4.4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <em id="algorithm1.4.4.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=1,\dots,M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=1,\dots,M</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm1.4.4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.5" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> <span id="algorithm1.4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">// </span><span id="algorithm1.4.5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Prompt phase </span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.6" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.4.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <em id="algorithm1.4.6.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in\mathcal{P}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in\mathcal{P}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm1.4.6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.7" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">4</span></span>
            compute <math id="algorithm1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(0,m)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(0,m)}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="algorithm1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(\ell,m)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(\ell,m)}</annotation></semantics></math> via (<a href="#S4.E3" title="In Inference in an autoregressive reasoning model. ‣ 4 Reasoning-Aware Compression ‣ Reasoning Models Can Be Accurately Pruned via Chain-of-Thought Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>)–(<a href="#S4.E4" title="In Inference in an autoregressive reasoning model. ‣ 4 Reasoning-Aware Compression ‣ Reasoning Models Can Be Accurately Pruned via Chain-of-Thought Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>);
append <math id="algorithm1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(\ell-1,m)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(\ell-1,m)}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="algorithm1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{\ell}^{\mathrm{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>P</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{\ell}^{\mathrm{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.8" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> <span id="algorithm1.4.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">// </span><span id="algorithm1.4.8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Decode phase </span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.9" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">5</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.4.9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <em id="algorithm1.4.9.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in\mathcal{D}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in\mathcal{D}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm1.4.9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.10" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">6</span></span>
            sample <math id="algorithm1.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="z_{t}^{(m)}\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid z^{(m)}_{0:t-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}^{(m)}\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid z^{(m)}_{0:t-1})</annotation></semantics></math> via (<a href="#S4.E2" title="In Inference in an autoregressive reasoning model. ‣ 4 Reasoning-Aware Compression ‣ Reasoning Models Can Be Accurately Pruned via Chain-of-Thought Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>);
compute <math id="algorithm1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(0,m)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(0,m)}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="algorithm1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(\ell,m)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(\ell,m)}</annotation></semantics></math> via (<a href="#S4.E3" title="In Inference in an autoregressive reasoning model. ‣ 4 Reasoning-Aware Compression ‣ Reasoning Models Can Be Accurately Pruned via Chain-of-Thought Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>)–(<a href="#S4.E4" title="In Inference in an autoregressive reasoning model. ‣ 4 Reasoning-Aware Compression ‣ Reasoning Models Can Be Accurately Pruned via Chain-of-Thought Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>);
append <math id="algorithm1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(\ell-1,m)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}^{(\ell-1,m)}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="algorithm1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{\ell}^{\mathrm{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>D</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{\ell}^{\mathrm{D}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">7</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.4.11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Phase II: Layer-wise compression</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.12" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">8</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.4.12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <em id="algorithm1.4.12.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=1,\dots,L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=1,\dots,L</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm1.4.12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.13" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">9</span></span>
            <math id="algorithm1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{\ell}^{\mathrm{RAC}}\leftarrow[\mathbf{X}_{\ell}^{\mathrm{P}},\mathbf{X}_{\ell}^{\mathrm{D}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>RAC</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>P</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>D</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{\ell}^{\mathrm{RAC}}\leftarrow[\mathbf{X}_{\ell}^{\mathrm{P}},\mathbf{X}_{\ell}^{\mathrm{D}}]</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.14" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.14.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">10</span></span>
            <math id="algorithm1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathbf{W}}_{\ell}\leftarrow\textsc{Prune}(\mathbf{W}_{\ell},\mathbf{X}_{\ell}^{\mathrm{RAC}},s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Prune</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>RAC</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathbf{W}}_{\ell}\leftarrow\textsc{Prune}(\mathbf{W}_{\ell},\mathbf{X}_{\ell}^{\mathrm{RAC}},s)</annotation></semantics></math>   <span id="algorithm1.4.14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">// </span><span id="algorithm1.4.14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">e.g., SparseGPT, WANDA</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.15" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.15.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">11</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.4.15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="algorithm1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{f}=\{\widehat{\mathbf{W}}_{1},\dots,\widehat{\mathbf{W}}_{L}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{f}=\{\widehat{\mathbf{W}}_{1},\dots,\widehat{\mathbf{W}}_{L}\}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>One-shot pruning</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Experimental Setup.</span> To test the effectiveness of reasoning-aware compression, we perform one-shot pruning on several open-source Qwen architectures (1.5B, 7B, 14B, 32B) which have been distilled from the DeepSeek-R1 model. Each model is pruned in one-shot with SparseGPT at layer-wise unstructured sparsity of 20%, 30%, 40% and 50% using 1M calibration tokens from: (i) the standard English–web C4 corpus, (ii) <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Open-R1-Math-220k</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">HuggingFace (2025)</a></cite> or <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Codeforces</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shanghaoran Quan (2025)</a></cite> problem statements without answers or reasoning traces (”prompt only”) and (iii) those prompts augmented with up to <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\text{max}}=8192" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>8192</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\text{max}}=8192</annotation></semantics></math> on–policy chain–of–thought tokens collected from each corresponding dense model, as described in Algorithm 1 (“RAC”). 
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For mathematical reasoning we use <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Math500</span> and report <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">acc@1:1</span>: the percentage of problems for which the model’s single most-confident prediction exactly matches the ground-truth answer (Top-1 accuracy).
For <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">code generation</span> we use the CodeGen evaluation harness and report <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">acc@1:16</span>, i.e. the percentage of cases in which the correct solution appears anywhere within the model’s top-16 predictions, irrespective of rank (Top-16 accuracy).
All evaluations are zero-shot with no additional few-shot examples, and a 32k output token budget. This closely follows the evaluation pipeline used by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI et al. (2025)</a></cite> and the open-source replication of the DeepSeek pipeline from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">HuggingFace (2025)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:83%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>DeepSeek-R1 <span id="S5.T1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Math500</span> <span id="S5.T1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">acc@1:1</span> under one-shot pruning.
Accuracy with standard error (SE) on the left, total evaluation time in minutes on the right. Best accuracy or fastest runtime in <span id="S5.T1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#00E000;">green</span>.</figcaption>
